{
    "id": "year7-english-modals-lesson-4-persuasion",
    "title": "Modal Verbs in Persuasive Rhetoric & Formal Writing",
    "description": "Apply modal verbs strategically in persuasive writing and formal rhetoric to adjust tone, project authority, and construct powerful arguments.",
    "duration": 30,
    "level": "Year 7",
    "slides": [
        {
            "id": "slide-1",
            "title": "Modality as a Rhetorical Tool",
            "subtitle": "Crafting Convincing Speeches and Essays",
            "layout": "hero",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "persuasion-intro-heading",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "The Power of Persuasive Modals",
                        "level": 2,
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "persuasion-intro-paragraph",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "In persuasive speeches and debate essays, modal verbs control how authoritative and urgent your argument sounds. Using **must** or **will** inspires immediate action, while **might** or **could** is useful for introducing soft counter-arguments.",
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "persuasion-rhetoric-table",
                    "type": "table",
                    "props": {
                        "rows": 4,
                        "columns": 3,
                        "data": [
                            [
                                "Rhetorical Goal",
                                "Target Modal Verbs",
                                "Persuasive Example"
                            ],
                            [
                                "Urgent Rallying Cry",
                                "Must / Will",
                                "We **must** act now to save our oceans."
                            ],
                            [
                                "Formal Complaint",
                                "Should / Ought to",
                                "The council **should** reconsider this decision."
                            ],
                            [
                                "Conceding a Counterpoint",
                                "Might / May",
                                "Opponents **might** argue that costs are high."
                            ]
                        ]
                    }
                }
            ]
        },
        {
            "id": "slide-2",
            "title": "Strengthening Argumentative Tone",
            "subtitle": "Transforming Weak Suggestions into Authoritative Claims",
            "layout": "split",
            "components": [
                {
                    "id": "tone-heading",
                    "type": "heading",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Upgrading Weak Sentences",
                        "level": 3,
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "tone-paragraph",
                    "type": "paragraph",
                    "props": {
                        "content": "Compare these two sentences:\n- **Weak**: 'We *could* try to reduce plastic waste.' (Tentative, unconvincing)\n- **Strong**: 'We *must* eliminate plastic waste.' (Authoritative, urgent)\n\nReplacing weak modal verbs with strong modals instantly boosts your argument's impact!",
                        "align": "left"
                    }
                },
                {
                    "id": "persuasion-fill-blank-1",
                    "type": "fillInTheBlank",
                    "props": {
                        "title": "Upgrade the Weak Sentences with Authoritative Modal Verbs",
                        "text": "1. Weak: We could plant more trees. -> Strong: We {{blank}} plant more trees to combat pollution.\n2. Weak: The school might fix the playground. -> Strong: The administration {{blank}} repair the playground immediately.\n3. Concession: While opponents {{blank}} disagree, our proposal remains the best option.",
                        "blanks": [
